Scot as our audience might have seen  this whole film was based on a Vanity  Fair article with one of the best  headlines ever roar shock and awe it was  about the two CIA investigators and  interrogators can you tell me a little  bit about what in that article set out  to you and at what point you realized  this story really needed to pivot and  become about Dan Jones instead well  first of all thank you everyone for  coming when I started I was sort of  drawn to these two psychologists in  large part because both of my parents  were psychologists and it was somewhat  stunning to me to think about the idea  that someone would weaponize psychology  and and there was no science to back up  the idea that you could do these  techniques to extract the truth out of  people I started my research and then  the report that Dan Jones wrote for the  Senate Intelligence Committee came out  and I started pouring over the report  with Jennifer and then I was able to  meet Daniel Jones and I went out to  dinner with him and obviously he can't  tell me anything that isn't in that  report so at some point I said to him  tell me the story of how you told the  story and I became really intrigued by  the process he had to go through and it  seemed to me to be more interesting to  write a story about a truth teller than  about you know two guys who basically  ripped us off Jennifer can you talk  about the timing of all of this because  you're trying to get this film made and  meanwhile history is also unfolding yeah  when we started working on the project  five years ago Obama was still in office  and the torture program was over and at  that point we felt an urgent need to  tell the story so that history didn't  repeat itself and to try and have a  truth told and as we went through the  process it just became more and more and  more irrelevant by the day and then on  the campaign trail we have a  presidential candidate saying he would  bring back torture or worse  and at that point it just became  incredibly apparent to us that there was  an extremely urgent need but then it  continued on through as we have been  witnessing in real time now need for  checks and balances and I we hope that  the film will shed some light on the  importance of that Adam you also got to  spend some time with the real Dan Jones  what are some of the things that struck  out the most to you about him as a human  being his sense of decorum as a Senate  staffer that which I related to that  there's definitely a chain of command  that he has to follow he couldn't just  you know he didn't know it was gonna be  that long when he started doing his job  but it's not his responsibility to go  into the higher-ups office and basically  demand that he be heard and give his  opinion about it could give his opinion  period he was to present fact and let  the fact facts speak for himself and I  and even his faith in spite of what he  was uncovering within the checks and  balances in those institutions are still  very strong yeah you know he still I  mean as it is in the movie there was a  way that he can go a fast track to  getting the information out but he still  has faith in this and I think the Scott  script is a good job of showing that it  isn't the institution's overall it so  it's really a question of you know  people in positions of power and  leadership and accountability it's not  an indictment of the CIA overall look  his people within the CIA obviously  spoke out against the program so he has  a very level head and methodical  approach to work and maybe could be  characterized as obsessive but you kind  of always hope that that kind of  personality is in those rooms  you know isolated especially obviously  in in in government you know following a  moral code presenting fact as a as  opposed to opinion yeah and Jon Hamm  what's fascinating to me about this film  is I feel like we live in a culture  where you're either right or you're  wrong and people see things and they see  things that one way and it's hard to see  things any other way and yet  mr. McDonough is a little bit more in  between I feel like I'm just curious  your approach and playing this film  especially when it came to good guy bad  guy how he might be portrayed um yeah I  think we're we're sort of indoctrinated  at this point in the political landscape  to feel like everything is one opposite  or the other polar opposite and there  and the reality is most functioning  functional politics takes place in  between and as my character in the movie  says politics is messy and democracy is  messy and that doesn't mean it doesn't  work you know one of my one of the  things I'm most proud about being in  this film is that this is an example of  government being applied correctly and  working it's not perfect but this the  truth did come out a version of it and  Dennis's job was to massage that truth  out to get it out in the most fair  effective way if he would have just  stomped his foot said no we're right but  it's gonna come out this way the other  side would have pushed back and they  would have got nowhere so we had he  realized he had there had to be a  give-and-take there had to be some  negotiation there and that's 95 percent  of politics and that's the way it's  designed and it's you know it's what  we're going through right now is as a  version of that the system doing what  working by the by the directions that  they set down on a piece of paper you  know 200 some odd years ago so that's  that's what I think is is interesting  about about dennis's is that you know he  knows that the pendulum swings and he  knows that the this administration is  only in the last four maybe eight years  and so he wants to work a much longer  period if you get as much done as you  can in the time that you have one thing  that was not in the script originally as  I understand it was the torture scenes  can you talk about the decisions there's  got to actually include that and then  once you did decide to include that how  you wanted to shoot those particular  scenes they're difficult to watch yeah I  mean initially I didn't I didn't want to  include them at all because I was  worried that it would take people out of  the movie  and and then I had a conversation when I  was doing my research with a guy named  Alberto Mora and he was the Navy's  general counsel when the program was on  worked in the Pentagon and Alberto when  he found out what was going on really  stood up and tried to stop it he is a  real hero in this story and when I  interviewed him he asked me the exact  same question you're asking now and said  how are you gonna handle that and I said  I don't know I don't really want to tip  over the experience for the audience by  overwhelming them with torture and he  said if you don't show it aren't you  doing exactly what the CIA did when they  destroyed the tapes and as painful as it  is I think it's important that you know  a lot of what went on here was about  language you know enhanced interrogation  you know II I T sounds like something  like a drop of retsyn or something like  that and it's torture that's why we have  that word and a word like waterboarding  is a word but when you see what that  really means to a human being that's a  very different experience and you know  we work in a visual medium and we have  to understand what really went on here  so I guess I felt like if I didn't do it  I was I was compounding sort of the  original sin here and you know and once  I decided to do it I also really was  struck by by John McCain's speech which  closes the film which is you know it's  about us not about them so when Greg  O'Brien and I started editing the movie  I was like for every two shots of  someone being tortured or every shot of  someone being tortured what's trying to  have two shots of the torturers and and  and you know make it clear that the  people were doing this we're doing it  with masks on and I just find it  fascinating that you think you're  serving justice but you have to do it  with the ski mask on and that was  probably the thing that we spent the  most time on in post-production really  finding just the right balance where we  weren't gratuitous but we also felt like  we were serving the truth and when you  got it very closely you'll notice that  what you see is a foot shaking water  dripping on the ground sound that we're  telling a lot of the story without very  directly showing what it is and it's  still pretty terrifying my thanks to Jon  Hamm Adam driver Jennifer Fox and Scott  Z burns
 

